Marcin Bugaj: Okay, can you see my slides and can you hear my voice?

159
00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:29.869
ignacio: Yeah. Yes. Yeah.

160
00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:38.809
Marcin Bugaj: Okay, great. So, hi. A short update on Project 3, which is zkVM guest API standardization.

161
00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:43.550
Marcin Bugaj: Today it's a recap, one new proposal.

162
00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:45.870
Marcin Bugaj: And a handover.

163
00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:58.700
Marcin Bugaj: We have now 10 accepted standards. They cover the ISA, input and output, crypto accelerators, termination, memory layout.

164
00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:06.670
Marcin Bugaj: The safety traps, the static library, and two standards accepted since the last call.

165
00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:11.200
Marcin Bugaj: ELF loading and validation, and accelerated memory operations.

166
00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:17.049
Marcin Bugaj: One standard is open for your review.

167
00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:28.679
Marcin Bugaj: That is the one in amber. Four standards are on hold and are not mentioned on this, on this board. I can come back to them, later.

168
00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:42.090
Marcin Bugaj: The new one is proving cost estimation. Proving is slow, far too slow to run, after every change in the client program.

169
00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:46.469
Marcin Bugaj: But the emulator already visits every instruction.

170
00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:50.620
Marcin Bugaj: So it can add up the work the prover would have to do.

171
00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:56.000
Marcin Bugaj: And the result could be a single number, we call it C.

172
00:21:56.260 --> 00:22:00.959
Marcin Bugaj: And the expectation is that, proving time

173
00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:04.980
Marcin Bugaj: is linear to C, as reported by the emulator.

174
00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:16.780
Marcin Bugaj: When C goes down, proving gets faster, and that holds on any hardware, CPU, or GPU, because C measures work rather than time.

175
00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:25.669
Marcin Bugaj: The second feature of that standard is cost scopes, which narrow the measurement to one region of a program.

176
00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:30.900
Marcin Bugaj: So the cost of that region can be read on its own.

177
00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:43.460
Marcin Bugaj: Yeah, and some people raised a concern that there must be a linear relationship between this number, C, and the proving time, so I think that it

178
00:22:43.460 --> 00:23:02.310
Marcin Bugaj: It can be semi-linear in the sense that it approximately has to be linear for the practical purposes of judging whether a change improved the approving time, or improved the performance, or the performance deteriorated as a result of…

179
00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:03.960
Marcin Bugaj: Changes in the code.

180
00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:14.700
Marcin Bugaj: So please review the standard. It's quite a massive one, and requires a lot of work from the KVMs, so yeah.

181
00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:29.400
Marcin Bugaj: Yeah, and the fourth slide is Hanover. I'm leaving the ethereum Foundation at the end of the month, and ownership of the standards, goes to cody.

182
00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:36.749
Marcin Bugaj: My personal email is on the slide, please feel free to reach out.

183
00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:37.760
Marcin Bugaj: Yep.

184
00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:44.959
Marcin Bugaj: And the next, thing is,

185
00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:50.119
Marcin Bugaj: Standard, which is in the draft mode, minimum memory resources.

186
00:23:50.390 --> 00:24:04.670
Marcin Bugaj: it sets a floor on how much stack and heap a zkVM must give to the guest. Without those numbers, a guest that runs on one ZKVM can run out of memory on another.

187
00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:22.540
Marcin Bugaj: The standard is blocked on one thing. Nobody has profiled a demanding guest against a large mainnet block, so the numbers are still blank. I've heard that Han performed some benchmarks, so maybe we would be able to fill in

188
00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:24.629
Marcin Bugaj: The missing numbers soon.

189
00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:36.870
Marcin Bugaj: U256 acceleration. In April, I benchmarked this on zk and saw little or no advantage.

190
00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:48.460
Marcin Bugaj: But a few weeks ago, ZSK provided its own benchmarks with a malmod opcode acceleration, and that gave about 40% better proving on some blocks.

191
00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:53.700
Marcin Bugaj: And those two results don't contradict each other,

